# 14. APPROVAL AND PERMISSION SYSTEM

Build a visible approval workflow.

Every proposed action must show:
- What will happen.
- Which device will be affected.
- The intended new state.
- Why the action is needed.
- Its risk level.
- Whether approval is required.

Use the following default policy:

LOW RISK:
Reading device state may proceed without approval.

MEDIUM RISK:
Turning lights on or off and changing the thermostat may be executed automatically only when the user has enabled that specific automation policy. Otherwise, require approval.

HIGH RISK:
Unlocking doors must always require explicit approval for that specific action.

Disabling security protections, changing access permissions, or performing similarly consequential operations must not be enabled by default.

Never interpret a general command as blanket authorization for unrelated consequential actions.

Implement:
- Approve selected action.
- Approve all eligible actions in the displayed plan.
- Reject selected action.
- Cancel execution.
- Revoke pending approval before execution.

Approval of one action must not approve a different action with changed parameters.

Bind approvals to the specific plan version and action arguments.

Any material plan change must invalidate affected approvals.

Show pending approvals prominently.

The simulator may support unlocking a door after explicit approval, but it must never unlock the door merely because the AI says doing so would be convenient.

